# Runner Setup, 3-Tier Target Authority, and Revision Rules

<!-- GEN: Why: wrong runner, wrong target database, or unreviewed autogenerated DDL is the most expensive migration mistake. Kind: Mandatory. -->

> Topic: Inspecting runner configuration, 3-tier environment authority (`Local/Disposable` vs `Develop on user request` vs `Staging/Production by DevOps`), autogenerate hand-review, and revision rules (`M1`–`M3`, `M5`, `M13`).

Rules **M1**, **M2**, **M3**, **M5**, and **M13**.

---

## 1. Runner Configuration & Command Prefix (`M1`, `M3`)

Record these baseline facts in `codebase-notes.md` before authoring or running any migration:
- **Command Prefix & Working Directory**: `{{cmd_prefix}}` executed from `{{target_dir}}`.
- **Runner Config & Metadata**: `{{runner_files}}`.
- **Forbidden Delivery Commands (`M3`)**: Never use `{{forbidden_commands}}` to deliver schema or data changes.

| Purpose | Command (`cwd: {{target_dir}}`) | Allowed Target |
|---|---|---|
| **Check current revision / heads** | `{{status_cmd}}` | Local/Disposable, or `develop` when requested |
| **Autogenerate new revision** | `{{create_cmd}}` | Local/Disposable DB at head |
| **Apply / Rollback (`upgrade` / `downgrade`)** | `{{apply_and_revert_cmd}}` | Tier 1 (autonomous) or Tier 2 (`develop` **only on explicit user request**) |
| **Emit offline SQL (`--sql`)** | `{{offline_sql_cmd}}` | Safe offline — attach output to `migration-handoff.md` |

